Four attempts at the same problem, 200 tickets

  1. rules, first draft     81.5%   twenty minutes' work
  2. the model              78.0%   from the previous listing
  3. rules, second draft    95.5%   twenty minutes more
  4. rules + model on the rest  98.5%

Hybrid over 200 tickets
  rules answered            191 (96%)
  model answered            9 (4%)
  accuracy                  98.5%
  seconds                   1.8

  rules alone               95.5%
  model calls avoided       96% of them

What the model was asked to do — only the cases rules could not read:
  Returns   (truth Quality  ) El producto MRD-SAN-047 llegó dañado. Necesitamos 
  Billing   (truth Billing  ) Die Rechnung INV-91472 wurde doppelt berechnet.
  Billing   (truth Billing  ) Die Rechnung INV-94718 wurde doppelt berechnet.
  Returns   (truth Quality  ) El producto MRD-CLE-007 llegó dañado. Necesitamos 
  Quality   (truth Quality  ) El producto MRD-CLE-009 llegó dañado. Necesitamos 

The rules are not better than the model, and the model is not better than the
rules. Each is better at a different part of the same problem, and the cheap
one can tell when it is out of its depth. That is a design, not a compromise.
